{
  "name": "VU Peak Hold",
  "notes": "The bar and the marker that hangs above it. There is no peak-hold node and there does not need to be: two `audio_band` sources on the SAME bins with wildly different `smoothing` are a fast reading and a slow one, and a slow follower above a fast bar is what a peak hold looks like. 0.01 s against 2.0 s -- the top of the declared range -- plus `slew` 1.2 on the slow one, so it falls at a fixed speed rather than decaying exponentially.\n\nIt is not a true peak hold and the difference is worth stating: a real one latches the maximum, this one is an average, so it sits a little below a sharp transient and rises as well as falls. What it gets right is what matters -- it lags, it does not chase, and it decays visibly.\n\nThe chain makes it behave like a meter with a ballistic mode switch. The FAST band drives the SLOW band's `slew`, so a loud passage makes the marker fall quickly and a quiet one makes it hang almost still -- an automatic release. A third row runs the fast band into `M1.smoothing` as well, so under sustained level the two detectors converge and the marker rides the bar, then separates the moment the music stops. Jade through cobalt for the bar with one vermilion at the top of the scale, and the marker on chartreuse -- the one hue in neither family, so it is always readable against whatever it is sitting on.",
